# folds.topic -- folding loops (FOLD-LOOPS-MILESTONE-1.md): the construct and
# the diagnostics that are its own.

@topic concept.folds
title: Folds: a loop that carries one value, `with intermediate acc INITIAL`
summary: A fold iterates like a loop but, instead of collecting its iterations' values, carries one value (its intermediate) from each iteration to the next and is that value at the end.
index: concepts
alias: fold; reduce; accumulate with a loop; fold loop; folding loop; carry a value through a loop; with intermediate; running value
keywords: fold, reduce, accumulate, accumulator, intermediate, carry, sum, count, running, state, scanner, aggregate
related: concept.loops concept.lockstep task.accumulate capability.map-filter rule.immutable-bindings error.fold-intermediate-type error.affine-fold-unsupported

== WHAT IT IS
    fold x in xs with intermediate acc INITIAL:      every element
    fold i from a to b with intermediate acc INITIAL:   (and through,
                                                         down from)
    fold x in xs and i from 0 to n with intermediate ...  lockstep
    fold with intermediate acc INITIAL:              until a break

The clauses are a loop's, word for word. `acc` is the intermediate: in
each iteration it is the value the previous iteration produced (INITIAL
for the first), an immutable binding of the body. The body's value
becomes the next intermediate. The fold itself is the last intermediate.

    loop x in xs:                      the List of the body's values
        transform(x)
    fold x in xs with intermediate a initial:
        transform(a, x)                the final intermediate

== RULES
  * The loop's operands are evaluated first, in written order, then
    INITIAL, exactly once, in the enclosing scope; then the iterations.
  * An empty fold is INITIAL. `continue` keeps the intermediate for that
    iteration; a bare `break` ends the fold with the current intermediate
    (the one the iteration started with). Errors and `return` leave the
    fold as they leave any loop.
  * The intermediate is not in scope in INITIAL or the loop's operands, nor
    after the fold. Binding its name again in the body's own scope is a
    DUPLICATE (it is immutable); a nested scope may shadow it.
  * The fold's type is the intermediate's: the join of INITIAL's type and
    every normal completion of the body. A join of two different kinds
    is error.fold-intermediate-type; `continue` and `break` produce no
    value, they carry the intermediate unchanged.
  * A fold is a statement value, exactly where a loop or an `if` may be:
    the right side of `=`, after `return`, a body's last expression --
    never a call argument. Outside that position `fold` is an ordinary
    name.
  * An affine intermediate is error.affine-fold-unsupported.

== WHY
Iteration, aggregation and construction are different operations. A
collecting loop builds a List; a fold carries one value and builds
nothing it does not compute, so a count or a sum needs no List and no
mutable cell: natively the intermediate is one register carried around
the loop.

== COST OF A LIST OR STRING INTERMEDIATE
A List (or String) intermediate that the body only extends --
`acc.append(x)`, `s.concat(x)`, an `if` that appends or keeps it,
`continue`, `break` -- is carried natively as a construction plan: each
append extends it in place and the List is built once, when the fold
yields it (exhaustion or a break), so appending N elements copies O(N)
elements, as the same List carried by a self tail call does. The values
are exactly the eager fold's. It applies when, on every path of an
iteration, the intermediate is used at most once (a `continue` or
`break` counts as a use: it passes the intermediate on) and is never
captured by a function made in the body. Otherwise -- `n = acc.length()`
before `acc.append(n)`, an append computed before a `break`, both `acc`
and `acc.append(x)` kept -- the fold runs eagerly and each append copies
the List so far. A struct intermediate's List fields are always copied.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
fn total(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    fold x in xs with intermediate sum 0:
        sum + x

fn count_evens(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    fold x in xs with intermediate n 0:
        if x.mod(2) == 1:
            continue
        n + 1

[total([1, 2, 3]), total([]), count_evens([1, 2, 3, 4])]
@caption A sum, an empty fold (its initial value) and a count that skips with continue
@value [6, 0, 2]
@end

@example accept
fn first_run(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    fold x in xs with intermediate longest 0:
        if x < 0:
            break
        longest + 1

fn state(text: List[int]) -> int:
    s = fold c in text with intermediate st {depth: 0, max: 0}:
        d = st.depth + c
        if d > st.max:
            {depth: d, max: d}
        else:
            {depth: d, max: st.max}
    s.max

[first_run([4, 5, -1, 6]), state([1, 1, -1, 1, 1, -1])]
@caption break ends a fold with its current intermediate; a struct intermediate carries several values
@value [2, 3]
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn evens_scaled(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    fold x in xs with intermediate acc []:
        if x.mod(2) == 1:
            continue
        acc.append(x * 10)

fn chunks(xs: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
    fold x in xs with intermediate done []:
        if x == 0:
            done
        else:
            done.append([x, -x])

[evens_scaled([1, 2, 3, 4]), std::list::flat(chunks([1, 0, 2]))]
@caption Lists built by folds that only extend their intermediate: each append extends a construction plan, the List is built once
@value [[20, 40], [1, -1, 2, -2]]
@end

@example reject
fn f(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    fold x in xs with intermediate acc 0:
        if x > 0:
            acc + x

f([1])
@caption An if without else completes the body with unit when its condition is false
@error CORE SEMANTIC FOLD-INTERMEDIATE-TYPE
@at 3:9
@end

