@topic error.fold-intermediate-type
title: FOLD-INTERMEDIATE-TYPE: a fold's intermediate would change its kind
summary: A fold carries one value, of one type, from each iteration to the next; its initial value and some normal completion of its body have no common type but the dynamic one.
index: errors
alias: the intermediate of this fold is; have no common type
keywords: fold, intermediate, type, join, initial value, unit, else, struct
related: concept.folds concept.if-expressions error.type
code: CORE SEMANTIC FOLD-INTERMEDIATE-TYPE

== MEANING
A fold's intermediate has one type: the join of its initial value's type
and the type of every way its body completes normally. Unlike an `if`,
whose joined value is read once, the intermediate is read by the same body
again on the next iteration, so a join that only the dynamic type covers
-- an Int and a String, two different named structs, anonymous structs of
different fields, a value and the `unit` of an `if` without `else` -- is
rejected where the body first completes with the other type:

    the intermediate "acc" of this fold is int (its initial value), but
    this completion of the body is str

The diagnostic's related locations are the fold and its initial value.
Nothing that only follows from the conflict (a field read of the
intermediate, a use of the fold's value) is reported beside it.

== WHAT TO DO
Make every normal completion of the body a value of the intermediate's
type: give an `if` its `else` (usually the unchanged intermediate), build
the same struct fields in every branch, or convert the value. A path that
should leave the intermediate as it is can `continue`; one that should end
the fold can `break`.

@example reject
fn f(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    fold x in xs with intermediate acc 0:
        "no"

f([1])
@caption A String completion of an Int fold
@error CORE SEMANTIC FOLD-INTERMEDIATE-TYPE
@at 3:9
@end

@example accept
fn f(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    fold x in xs with intermediate acc 0:
        if x > 1:
            acc + x
        else:
            acc

f([1, 2, 3])
@caption Every completion an Int: the else keeps the intermediate
@value 5
@end

@topic error.affine-fold-unsupported
title: AFFINE-FOLD-UNSUPPORTED: a fold cannot carry an affine value
summary: An affine value (a coroutine handle, or a struct holding one) cannot be a fold's initial value or body value: carrying it would move it from each iteration into the next.
index: errors
alias: which would become the intermediate of a fold
keywords: fold, affine, coroutine, handle, ownership, intermediate
related: concept.folds concept.affine concept.coroutines error.affine-list-operation-unsupported
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-FOLD-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
A fold binds its intermediate anew in every iteration, to the value the
previous iteration completed with. For an affine value that is a move from
one iteration's binding into the next, with a release on every way the
fold can be left; the ownership analysis has no rule for it yet, so the
fold is rejected where the affine value would enter its intermediate: its
initial value, or a value its body completes with.

== WHAT TO DO
Carry an unrestricted value (a count, a state struct without the handle)
and keep the affine value outside the fold, or move it with a loop or a
function of its own.

@example reject
import std::coroutine

fn gen() -> int:
    yield 1
    2

fn f(n: int) -> int:
    coroutine {step: h} = gen()
    c = fold i from 0 to n with intermediate c h:
        c
    0

f(2)
@caption A coroutine handle as a fold's intermediate
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-FOLD-UNSUPPORTED
@at 9:48
@end
